WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:02.870
LIZ FONG-JONES: Hi there.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:03.580
My name is Liz.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.800
And I am an SRE teaching
Google Cloud customers

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.351
how to build and operate
reliable services.

00:00:08.351 --> 00:00:10.600
SETH VARGO: And I'm Seth, a
Developer Advocate focused

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.060
on infrastructure
and operations.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.000
In the previous episode,
we discussed SLOs and error

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.270
budgets as a means of balancing
availability and new feature

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.440
development.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.620
In this episode, Liz
and I discuss toil.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.630
So Liz, from what
I understand, toil

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.220
is just work that
I don't like to do,

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.480
so emails, meetings,
commuting to work.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.635
So what can I do
to reduce my toil?

00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:35.260
LIZ FONG-JONES: Hold
on a second, Seth.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.660
Those things aren't
specifically toil.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.687
So we call those
things "overhead,"

00:00:39.687 --> 00:00:41.770
because they are things
that are not directly tied

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.390
to a production service.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.720
So they're just a requirement
of any office, job really.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.670
Most people at the
company, not just SREs,

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.580
have to do expense
reports and so forth.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.390
And that's not really toil.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.150
SETH VARGO: OK, so if toil
isn't just work that I don't

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.359
like to do, what is it, then?

00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:57.983
LIZ FONG-JONES: Toil's
the kind of work

00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:00.550
that's tied to running a
production service that

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.569
tends to be manual, repetitive,
automatable, tactical,

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.280
and devoid of long-term value.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.590
Additionally, toil
tends to scale linearly

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.640
as the service grows.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.390
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:16.060
So each time an operator needs
to touch a production system,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.640
that represents some toil time.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.359
But what if I codify all of the
times that I need to touch it,

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:22.150
and I just run a script
instead of manually

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:23.025
running the commands?

00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:24.733
LIZ FONG-JONES: You've
reduced the amount

00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:27.190
of toil you have to do, but
you haven't made it not toil.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.940
Because you still have to run
that script every single time

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.440
the system requires
an intervention.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.360
So that still is manual.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.580
And it gets even worse the
larger your service grows,

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:37.955
because you have
to keep doing it

00:01:37.955 --> 00:01:39.520
over and over and over again.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.290
SETH VARGO: OK.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:41.873
And you also said
something-- the task

00:01:41.873 --> 00:01:43.000
has to be repetitive, too.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.100
So if I just do it
maybe once or twice,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.530
is that still toil or no?

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.330
LIZ FONG-JONES: That
is probably not toil,

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.760
at least the first or
second time, or at least

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.490
it's not toil that's
worth automating.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.072
You really want to automate
toil when it gets to the point

00:01:57.072 --> 00:01:58.780
that you're doing it
three or four times,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.300
or where you are taking hours
and hours out of your day.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.050
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.810
So if a task can be
automated, it probably

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.280
should be automated.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.620
What about things that are
pretty common in operations,

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.349
like pager alerts?

00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:12.640
LIZ FONG-JONES: So pager
alerts are almost always toil,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.710
because of the fact that
they are interrupt driven

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:15.560
and they're reactive.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.930
You don't know when
it's going to happen.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.846
And it just swallows a
bunch of your time just

00:02:18.846 --> 00:02:21.080
to get the system back
to where it was before.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.500
SETH VARGO: OK, so what if
I get paged in the middle

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.420
of the night to provision
additional capacity

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.839
for a service?

00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:29.380
So I look through the system,
and I realize that the only way

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.480
to do this is manually.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.480
I have to click buttons.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.040
I have to run commands.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:33.857
So I do that.

00:02:33.857 --> 00:02:35.440
I get the service
back up and running.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.080
And I go back to sleep.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.950
And in the morning, I
wake up, and I realize

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.610
that I need to automate this.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.780
So I spent about six or seven
hours building some automation

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.840
so that the service can scale
automatically in the future.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.010
That's a lot of toil.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.084
How do we account for that?

00:02:50.084 --> 00:02:52.000
LIZ FONG-JONES: Not
quite, because of the fact

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.730
that the time that you spent
during the middle of the night,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.410
between when you
got paged and when

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.110
the system returned to normal
from your manual action,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:02:59.980
that's toil.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.532
However, the latter part of
what you did during the day--

00:03:02.532 --> 00:03:04.240
that's a worthwhile
project that actually

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.540
reduces the toil over
time, because the toil has

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:08.650
no enduring value.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.630
But the automation
that you do in order

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.140
to reduce the amount of toil
you do, that's real useful work.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:15.920
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.470
And I think it's clear now
why you've coined a new term

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.754
to define this type of work.

00:03:19.754 --> 00:03:21.920
There's always going to be
tasks that you don't want

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.230
to do, like overhead,
like email, and meetings,

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.150
and commuting, and
expense reports.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.030
And there's always going to be
kind of operationally grungy

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.390
tasks, like restarting
services, that

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.470
are devoid of long-term value.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.492
So they aren't actually
toil in certain cases.

00:03:37.492 --> 00:03:39.950
In previous episodes, we talked
about how everything in SRE

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.340
is measured, though.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.712
So how do we measure toil?

00:03:42.712 --> 00:03:45.170
LIZ FONG-JONES: So there are
two ways that we measure toil.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.210
First of all, we try
to concentrate the toil

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.500
into blocks, for instance,
during your on-call week,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.110
so you don't get
pages and tickets off

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:51.984
of your on-call week.

00:03:51.984 --> 00:03:54.000
So it makes it a lot
easier to measure

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.100
when it is toil,
when it is not toil.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.210
Additionally, we have people
track their toil time,

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.650
sometimes by sampling.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.720
And we send quarterly
surveys to make sure

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.660
that people understand how much
of their time is spent on toil.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.610
Do they feel like
they have more room?

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.719
And then toil is also
everyone's responsibility.

00:04:09.719 --> 00:04:12.260
So we'll often ask the product
development software engineers

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.926
to measure their
amount of toil as well,

00:04:13.926 --> 00:04:16.270
to make sure that we're
spending everyone's time well.

00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.019
SETH VARGO: So you
mentioned that the goal

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.910
should be to reduce toil.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.262
Shouldn't the goal be to
eliminate toil entirely?

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.720
LIZ FONG-JONES:
Part of the problem

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.860
with eliminating toil entirely
is that there are often tasks

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.600
that are not worth automating.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.614
If you spend an hour
fixing something by hand,

00:04:32.614 --> 00:04:34.280
and it happens maybe
three times a year,

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.637
but it would take you 10
hours to write the automation,

00:04:36.637 --> 00:04:38.720
that's going to have a
very long payoff threshold.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.780
Whereas if you have something
that's firing all the time,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.300
you might want to go and
get it out of your way.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.850
So as you reduce
the amount of toil

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.727
and leave alone the things
that are not worth automating,

00:04:49.727 --> 00:04:51.560
you'll find that there's
some amount of toil

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.120
associated with each service.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.800
You can keep the
total amount of toil

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.010
you do somewhere between
30% and 50% of your time,

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.790
and spend the rest of your time
doing worthwhile projects that

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.570
reduce the toil, so
that over time, you

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.970
can accept more responsibility
for more services,

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.394
or your service can just
naturally grow in size

00:05:07.394 --> 00:05:09.560
and have your amount of
work really remain constant.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.470
SETH VARGO: I see.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.280
So SREs get to
spend at least half,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.490
if not two thirds of their time
working on real engineering

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:16.990
work.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:20.136
The SRE stands for Site
Reliability Engineering.

00:05:20.136 --> 00:05:22.010
So they get to focus on
things like improving

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.070
reliability, performance,
utilization, monitoring.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.570
And all of those
things can actually

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.617
reduce toil in the
future as well.

00:05:28.617 --> 00:05:29.450
LIZ FONG-JONES: Yes.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.710
That's exactly correct.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.600
And also, we share
our best practices.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.050
So when we do these
items of research

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.060
into how we make
our services better,

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.730
we can often apply them across
multiple different SRE teams.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.800
So we'll publish white
papers within the company

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.430
or on our blog, describing to
SREs how they might make sure

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.010
that their toil stays
low and within budget,

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.710
so that we can spend more time
making our services better

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.030
in the long term.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.242
At the end of the day, the E
in SRE stands for Engineering.

00:05:57.242 --> 00:05:58.700
And engineering
work is really what

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.860
lets our organization
scale, and meet

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.440
the demands of all of the
applications and services

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.820
and machines that we support.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:05.570
SETH VARGO: Great.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:06.778
This has been really helpful.

00:06:06.778 --> 00:06:09.200
And I think I have a much
better understanding of toil

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.120
and its negative consequences.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.840
LIZ FONG-JONES: But toil isn't
always entirely negative.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.430
Sometimes, it's
worthwhile to have

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.490
some amount of predictable
and automatable work

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.052
for individual people
to cut their teeth on.

00:06:22.052 --> 00:06:24.260
For instance, let's suppose
that you have someone new

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.060
to your team that
wants to learn how

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.526
does the system actually work
before you put them on call.

00:06:28.526 --> 00:06:30.650
Well, you might have them
poke around at the system

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:33.410
manually in order to fix
something that you already

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.640
had lying around.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.320
Also, it's really
helpful to have things

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.278
that people feel satisfied
by doing, where they

00:06:38.278 --> 00:06:40.670
get an immediate reward,
rather than working

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.550
at a product for the long term
and not necessarily knowing

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.110
when it's going to finish.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.540
So sometimes, it's
helpful for people

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.040
to do those tasks
in the short term.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.960
But we don't think it's
a good idea for people

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.960
to focus entirely on doing toil,
because that doesn't really

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.240
advance the engineering
part of their job,

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.700
and it can cause career
stagnation and unhappiness

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.870
in the long term.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.370
SETH VARGO: So toil
and toil budgets

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.710
do not have a direct relation
to the DevOps pillars

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.750
that we've discussed
in previous episodes.

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.940
But they do closely
influence the desire

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:11.600
to measure everything and
reduce organizational silence.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.120
By giving operators a
quantitative measurement,

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.340
toil and toil budgets
ensure a balance

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.310
between administering the
system and improving it.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:20.940
This has really helped a lot.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.490
I think it's a lot
clearer now why

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:25.571
we say that cost SRE
implements DevOps.

00:07:25.571 --> 00:07:27.570
LIZ FONG-JONES: Thank you
everyone for watching.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.278
Don't forget to
subscribe to the channel.

00:07:29.278 --> 00:07:31.580
And also stay tuned for
our next video, where

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.700
we discuss how Google teaches
SRE practices to customers

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:38.260
through the Customer
Reliability Engineering Program.

